All the information is in place. Now I'll write the final `index.html` — a polished, self-contained version.

Design decisions (visuals are up to me, modern and clean):
- System font stack (no external fonts).
- Landing: dark blue / slate theme? Let's go with a bright, professional look: white / light gray background, deep blue accents (#1F5BD6 is the original's brand blue). The DISC letters get their own colors: D — red/orange, I — yellow/amber, S — green, C — blue. These are the classic DISC colors.
- Cards with subtle borders / shadows.
- Modal: white, rounded corners, 8-step tab bar (scrollable on mobile), 3-card grid (responsive: 3 → 1 column).
- Items: numbered ranks (display of 1–4), with a handle on the right side.

Wait, should I display the rank numbers? They're not shown on the original (from the CSS, `.disc-item` has no ::before number — let me double-check… no `.disc-item::before` with a number is defined. The list items are plain). But the "Я наиболее / Я наименее" framing conveys the meaning of the ordering. Showing rank numbers (1..4) improves UX and doesn't change the behavior. Let me add a subtle rank number that updates live during reordering. This is an improvement in my discretion. Hmm — "the test data and interface behavior is the same as the original" — behavior must be the same; the visuals are up to me. Rank numbers are a visual / UX addition and don't change behavior. OK, include them.

- Progress bar at the bottom (same semantics: (step+1)/8).
- Buttons: Назад / Далее / Завершить тест.
- After finishing → the modal switches to the results panel: "Ваш DISC-профиль" + large `D:…` display. Display each letter with its own color and value, plus the plain string too. Plus a disclaimer note. Buttons: "Пройти заново", "Закрыть".

Drag & drop details:

```
const COARSE = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches;
```

- pointerdown:
  - item = closest('.disc-item'); handle = e.target.closest('.disc-handle');
  - if (COARSE && !handle) return; // only the handle
  - if fine pointer: allow the whole item (but ignore if e.target is a button or a? there are no buttons inside items).
  - Primary button only (e.button === 0 or touch/pen).
  - Capture: item.setPointerCapture? Better to attach the listener to window.
- Lift:
  - rect = item.getBoundingClientRect();
  - ghost = item.cloneNode(true); ghost.classList.add('disc-ghost'); remove the handle? Keep the handle for appearance. position:fixed; left=rect.left; top=rect.top; width=rect.width; height=rect.height;
  - Append to body. item.classList.add('is-ghost') → visibility:hidden (keep the space).
  - offset = {x: e.clientX - rect.left, y: e.clientY - rect.top}
  - Start threshold: on coarse, don't lift until movement exceeds 6px (to avoid accidental drags and preserve scroll). For fine: lift immediately? The original Sortable fallbackTolerance is 6 for handle, 0 otherwise. Let's do: fine → lift immediately on pointerdown (cursor grab). coarse → 8px threshold; if not lifted by the point the pointer exceeds the threshold… but once a pointerdown on the handle is captured, a small scroll won't happen (touch-action:none on the handle). That's enough: on coarse + handle, lift immediately on pointerdown, but require a movement threshold before visual lift? Simpler: lift immediately; prevent scroll (touch-action none on handle). Let's use an 8px threshold for both (a tiny drag feels right; and with lift-on-threshold, the item stays in the flow so you can still scroll the list). Actually, with lift-on-threshold: while below the threshold, keep the pointer listener; the user can still scroll the list (touch-action is none only on the handle… with touch-action none on the handle, scrolling from the handle is blocked — which is what the original does: `.disc-handle{touch-action:none}` and the whole list is only during reordering).

Hmm wait, in Sortable with forceFallback on mobile, dragging is only from the handle, with a 6px tolerance — the user can still scroll the list by touching the item body (not the handle). With pointer events + touch-action:none on the handle, touch on the handle: no scroll, drag. On touch on the body of the item: scroll is allowed (touch-action auto), but pointerdown fires; if the user actually scrolls, pointercancel fires and we abort.

Implementation with an 8px threshold for both (to feel natural and avoid accidental clicks):

```js
let drag = null;
function onPointerDown(e){
  if (drag) return;
  if (!e.isPrimary || (e.pointerType==='mouse' && e.button!==0)) return;
  const item = e.target.closest('.disc-item');
  if (!item) return;
  const handle = e.target.closest('.disc-handle');
  const needHandle = COARSE;
  if (needHandle && !handle) return;
  if (item.getAttribute('aria-disabled')) return;
  e.preventDefault(); // avoid text selection / image drag
  drag = {item, list: item.closest('.disc-list'), x0:e.clientX, y0:e.clientY, offset:null, ghost:null, lifted:false};
  window.addEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove, {passive:false});
  window.addEventListener('pointerup', onPointerUp);
  window.addEventListener('pointercancel', onPointerUp);
}
function lift(d){
  const item = d.item; const rect = item.getBoundingClientRect();
  d.ghost = item.cloneNode(true);
  d.ghost.classList.add('disc-item--lift');
  d.ghost.style.width = rect.width+'px';
  document.body.appendChild(d.ghost);
  d.offset = {x: d.x - rect.left, y: d.y - rect.top};
  item.classList.add('is-slot');
  positionGhost(d);
}
function onPointerMove(e){
  if (!drag) return;
  if (!e.isPrimary) return;
  if (!e.cancelable) return;
  if (!drag.lifted){
    const dx = e.clientX-drag.x0, dy = e.clientY-drag.y0;
    if (dx*dx+dy*dy < 64) return; // threshold 8px
    lift(drag);
    drag.lifted = true;
  }
  e.preventDefault();
  drag.x = e.clientX; drag.y = e.clientY;
  positionGhost(drag);
  // Determine insertion position
  const list = drag.list;
  const items = [...list.querySelectorAll('.disc-item')].filter(li => li !== drag.item);
  let before = null;
  for (const li of items){
    const r = li.getBoundingClientRect();
    if (drag.y < r.top + r.height/2){ before = li; break; }
  }
  if (before) list.insertBefore(drag.item, before); else list.appendChild(drag.item);
}
function onPointerUp(e){
  if (!drag) return;
  clean up listeners...
  const d = drag; drag = null;
  if (!d.lifted){ return; }
  d.item.classList.remove('is-slot');
  const slot = d.item; // current DOM position
  const ghost = d.ghost;
  // Insert the actual item at the ghost's position
  ghost.parentNode.insertBefore(slot, ghost);
  ghost.remove();
}
```

Edge case: if you drop outside the list (outside the ul) → append to the end. OK.

For visual drop feedback, add a drop slot indicator: when the item is `is-slot`, draw a highlighted gap.

Also add a subtle scale / shadow to the lifted ghost.

**Keyboard support** (an optional nice feature):
- Items are focusable (tabindex=0? that's 96 tab stops, though only the active step is visible — display none removes them from tab order, so at any moment only 12 items are in the active pane).
- Arrow keys: move the focused item up / down (swap with neighbor). Announce? aria-live for the list? Keep it simple: ArrowUp/ArrowDown on focus reorders, Home/End to jump. This is a bonus; the original is drag-only. Include it — low risk, and useful. Add aria-roledescription? Let's keep it minimal but accessible: `role="list"` on ul (native), `role="listitem"` on li (native). Add a `data-disc-list` hint.

**Score calculation** (exact same as the original):

```js
function computeScore(){
  const lists = [...document.querySelectorAll('.disc-list')];
  const res = {d:0,i:0,s:0,c:0};
  lists.forEach(ul=>{
    const lis=[...ul.querySelectorAll('.disc-item')];
    const t0 = lis[0].dataset.type;
    const t3 = lis[3].dataset.type;
    res[t0]+=1; res[t3]-=1;
  });
  return res;
}
```

Identical result to the original algorithm.

