Interesting! After move2, the order is [s, i, c, d] — the s-item (previously index 3) has moved to the front. ✓ (Since all coordinates are zero, my assumption about y=-1 was correct.)

After pointerup: 3 children [i,c,d] — the s-item disappeared from the list, but the ghost count is 0!

So in endDrag: `d.ghost.parentNode.insertBefore(pos, d.ghost)` — if the ghost's parent isn't body, or if ghost has already been removed, and insertBefore removed d.item from the list and ghost wasn't found... Let me think about it this way: insertBefore(node, ref) removes node from its current position. Then `d.ghost.remove()`. If the ghost was already removed at that point (e.g., double invocation of endDrag?), then insertBefore(pos, a ghost that's been removed from the DOM) — wait, if ghost is no longer in the DOM, `ghost.parentNode` is null → `null.insertBefore` should throw an exception... but no exception is being thrown.

Actually wait — what if endDrag runs twice (pointerup fires twice? No — one dispatch). Or maybe endDrag is fine: ghost.parentNode = body; body.insertBefore(item, ghost) moves item next before the ghost in body...

Oh no. I see the bug now. In endDrag:

```js
const pos = d.item;
d.ghost.parentNode.insertBefore(pos, d.ghost);
```

`d.ghost.parentNode` — body. body.insertBefore(item, ghost) — moves item from the list to body, right before the ghost. Then ghost.remove(). So the item ends up in body, not in the list!!

But wait — during `place()`, d.item was re-inserted into the list (`list.insertBefore(d.item, ref)`). So at the time of pointerup, item is a child of list. And ghost is a child of body. `d.ghost.parentNode.insertBefore(pos, d.ghost)` uses ghost as the reference — but the reference node and the parent must be the same parent! ghost is in body, item is in list → body.insertBefore(item, ghost) moves item into body before the ghost. Then ghost.remove() → item is orphaned in body.

In the earlier harness (harness.js), I manually moved items with insertBefore and never called endDrag, so this bug didn't surface. But in the actual pointer flow: at lift time the item stays in the list (only the ghost is cloned, and the item is hidden). During movement, place() inserts the item into the list live. So at endDrag time the item is already in the list at the target position! The "insert item at the ghost position" dance was for the case where we don't do live reinsertion.

Fix: in endDrag, just remove the ghost and the is-slot class; the item is already in place (due to live place()). However, if the drag is cancelled (pointercancel) or ended without place() having been called (lift just happened, item is in the list but hidden) — the item is still at the last valid position, so that's fine.

Edge case: lift happens on the first move; place is called right after lift in the same handler → item is in the list. In onPointerMove after lift: placeGhost(drag); place(drag) — yes, always called.

So endDrag becomes:

```js
function endDrag(){
  if (!drag) return;
  const d = drag; drag = null;
  window.removeEventListener(...);
  document.body.classList.remove('is-dragging');
  if (d.ghost) { d.ghost.remove(); }
  d.item.classList.remove('is-slot');
  updateRanks(d.list);
  ...
}
```

This is much simpler and correct. Also, the "focus on move completion" behavior for desktop is preserved.

Let me rewrite endDrag accordingly and rerun the tests.

